# Local Voice Conversation Agent — PRD v2

## Priorities (in order)

1. **Turn detection** — knows when you've stopped talking, doesn't cut you off, doesn't sit there waiting after you're done.
2. **Response speed** — feels like a conversation, not a walkie-talkie.
3. **Functionality** — tool calls, memory, laptop/agent orchestration actually work and are extensible.
4. **Expressiveness** — nice to have, not a design constraint. Default to whatever voice is fastest; don't trade latency for emotion.

This is a change from v1: expressiveness is explicitly deprioritized. Any component choice that trades latency or reliability for a more "human" voice is the wrong choice for this build.

## Goal

An always-available local conversational agent that:

- listens continuously, detects turns accurately, and responds with minimal delay;
- is interruptible without breaking state;
- remembers prior conversations via Hindsight (`default` bank);
- can call tools — web search, and local laptop/agent orchestration (files, shell, other agents);
- runs fully locally and on free/open-source components.

## Stack decision

**Modular cascaded pipeline, not a speech-native model.** A voice-to-voice model (Moshi-class) has the best theoretical latency floor and best turn-taking naturalness, but weaker, less deterministic tool-calling, and is harder to wire into MCP/Hindsight. Since functionality and tool reliability outrank expressiveness here, a cascaded STT → LLM → TTS pipeline with a strong turn-detection layer is the right call — same conclusion as v1, but now optimized purely for speed and correctness rather than naturalness.

Recommended v2:

- **Pipecat** for pipeline orchestration, interruption handling, and tool lifecycle.
- **Kyutai STT (Unmute)** for streaming transcription — kept specifically because it's tuned for low latency, not because of voice quality.
- **A dedicated turn-detection/VAD layer**, not just "wait for silence" — see below. This is the component most likely to make or break the "feels responsive" goal, so it gets more attention than in v1.
- **Kyutai TTS (Unmute) or Kokoro-82M** for output speech — pick whichever benchmarks faster on your hardware. Do **not** use Chatterbox or other emotion-tuned models; they're heavier and slower for no benefit against these priorities.
- **A local tool-calling LLM via Ollama** (Qwen3-class or gpt-oss-class, whichever is faster on your machine at acceptable tool-calling accuracy — benchmark both, pick by speed once accuracy is "good enough").
- **Hindsight MCP** for long-term memory.
- **MCP tools** for web search, local filesystem/shell control, and orchestrating other agents on the laptop.

## Recommended architecture

```text
Microphone → VAD/Turn Detector → Streaming STT → Tool-capable LLM → Streaming TTS → Speaker
                                                    │            ▲
                                                    └→ Hindsight + MCP tools ─┘
```

1. **Turn detection sits in front of STT, not just as a silence timer.** Use a semantic/endpoint-aware detector (not pure VAD) so the agent doesn't jump in on a mid-sentence pause and doesn't sit idle after you're clearly done. This is the single highest-leverage component for hitting priority #1.
2. **Streaming pipeline:** Pipecat moves audio frames, cancels speech on interruption, and streams LLM text straight into TTS as it's generated — start speaking before the full response is done generating.
3. **Agent controller:** local LLM gets stable transcript segments plus JSON tool schemas; decides respond / recall / tool-call.
4. **Tool results** return to the same text context before response generation — deterministic, no speech-native conditioning tricks.
5. **Memory writer:** after each exchange, store only durable facts/decisions/preferences — not raw audio, not every fragment.

## Components

| Component | Choice | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Orchestrator | Pipecat (Python) | Frame pipeline, interruption, tool lifecycle |
| Turn detection | Semantic endpointing model (e.g. a lightweight turn-detector alongside VAD) + Kyutai's "flush trick" | Accuracy and speed both matter more here than anywhere else in the pipeline |
| Streaming STT | Kyutai STT via Unmute | Fastest local streaming option with production latency numbers |
| Agent controller | Local tool-calling LLM via Ollama (MLX backend on Apple Silicon) | Native function calling; benchmark 2–3 models for speed/accuracy trade-off |
| Speech output | Kyutai TTS or Kokoro-82M via Unmute | Fastest local options; explicitly not optimizing for expressiveness |
| Memory | Hindsight MCP, `default` bank | `recall` before responses, `sync_retain` after durable facts |
| Tools | MCP clients | Web search; filesystem/shell control; sub-agent orchestration |
| Deferred | Moshi/MoshiRAG, Chatterbox-style emotional TTS | Only revisit if functionality and latency goals are already met and expressiveness becomes a priority later |

## Controller behavior

State machine, unchanged in shape from v1 but re-prioritized in tuning:

```text
LISTENING → THINKING → TOOL_RUNNING → SPEAKING
    ▲                         │           │
    └──────── interruption / cancellation ┘
```

- Never block audio ingestion on a tool call.
- Turn detector — not a fixed silence timer — decides when to hand off to STT/LLM. Tune this against real conversation samples (including your own pauses/filler words) before tuning anything else.
- Before personal questions, call Hindsight `recall` with transcript + session summary.
- For external/current info, run the selected tool call; if it's slow, let TTS give a short filler acknowledgement rather than going silent.
- On interruption or topic change, cancel stale generation and stale tool calls immediately — this is part of "quick response," not a separate feature.
- Inject only compact tool/memory results (~100–300 tokens) into the LLM context to keep response generation fast.
- Retain durable info asynchronously after turn completion, with provenance and project tags.

## Tool-call contract

Unchanged from v1 — it was already correctly designed for reliability over cleverness:

```json
{"type":"respond","text":"..."}
{"type":"tool_call","name":"memory.recall","arguments":{"query":"..."}}
{"type":"tool_call","name":"web.search","arguments":{"query":"..."}}
{"type":"tool_call","name":"laptop.run","arguments":{"command":"..."}}
```

Policy: read-only tools run automatically; writes, sends, purchases, and destructive/system-level actions require confirmation. Log call ID, transcript span, latency, cancellation, result size, errors — this logging is what lets you actually measure whether priorities #1–#2 are being hit, so treat it as required, not optional instrumentation.

## Build sequence

1. **Turn detection + audio baseline first.** Before wiring the LLM or TTS, get mic → VAD/turn-detector → STT solid: correct endpointing, no clipped starts/ends, echo cancellation working. This used to be step 1 of a generic voice baseline; now it's the explicit top priority and gets its own dedicated tuning pass against real speech samples, including your own pauses and filler words.
2. **Add LLM + TTS, measure round-trip latency.** Get the full loop running and benchmark end-to-end response time before adding any tools. This is your speed baseline — record it, since every later addition should be checked against it.
3. **One tool:** Add Hindsight `recall`; verify it doesn't blow your latency budget.
4. **Local orchestration tool:** Add filesystem/shell/sub-agent control via MCP, with the read-only-auto / write-needs-confirmation policy.
5. **Web tool:** Add structured web search with cancellation and a spoken filler for long calls.
6. **Memory writeback:** Post-turn extraction + `sync_retain`, with a strict durable-fact filter.
7. **Latency/functionality hardening:** profile the full pipeline, fix the worst offender each pass, repeat. Only after this is solid, optionally revisit expressiveness (Moshi experiment, richer TTS) as a separate, lower-priority track.

## First milestone

Say something with a natural mid-sentence pause and confirm the agent doesn't jump in. Then ask, "What did we decide about the memory architecture?", get a Hindsight-grounded response, then ask it to check something on the web or run a local command — all without restarting the conversation.

Target metrics (tightened from v1 since speed/turn-detection are now the explicit priority):

- turn detection doesn't cut off mid-sentence pauses in test samples, and doesn't leave >500ms dead air after a real turn end;
- interruption stops obsolete speech within 250ms;
- memory recall/tool dispatch begins within 300ms of a stable transcript;
- end-to-end round trip (end of user speech → start of agent speech) tracked and reported per turn, not just spot-checked;
- no memory write occurs without passing the durable-fact filter;
- tool-call success rate tracked per tool, since "functionality" is a named priority and needs a number attached to it, not just a vibe check.

